## Proof

We work in a coalgebra $(C, \Delta, \varepsilon)$ over a field $\mathbb{k}$, with the sigma notation $\Delta(c) = \sum_{(c)} c_{(1)} \otimes c_{(2)}$.

**Goal.** Show that for all $c \in C$,
$$\sum_{(c)} \varepsilon(c_{(2)}) \otimes \Delta(c_{(1)}) = \Delta(c),$$
where the left-hand side lives in $\mathbb{k} \otimes (C \otimes C)$ and is identified with $C \otimes C$ via the natural isomorphism $\mathbb{k} \otimes (C \otimes C) \cong C \otimes C$, $\lambda \otimes x \mapsto \lambda \cdot x$.

**Step 1: The right counit axiom.**

By the counit axiom, $(\mathrm{id} \otimes \varepsilon) \circ \Delta = \mathrm{id}$, so
$$\sum_{(c)} c_{(1)} \otimes \varepsilon(c_{(2)}) = c \otimes 1_{\mathbb{k}},$$
which, under the identification $C \otimes \mathbb{k} \cong C$, reads
$$\sum_{(c)} c_{(1)}\, \varepsilon(c_{(2)}) = c. \tag{1}$$

**Step 2: Apply $\Delta$ to both sides of (1).**

Since $\Delta$ is $\mathbb{k}$-linear,
$$\Delta\!\left(\sum_{(c)} c_{(1)}\, \varepsilon(c_{(2)})\right) = \sum_{(c)} \varepsilon(c_{(2)})\, \Delta(c_{(1)}), \tag{2}$$
because each $\varepsilon(c_{(2)}) \in \mathbb{k}$ is a scalar and $\Delta$ is linear, so $\Delta(\lambda \cdot x) = \lambda \cdot \Delta(x)$ for $\lambda \in \mathbb{k}$, $x \in C$.

Applying $\Delta$ to the right-hand side of (1) gives
$$\Delta(c). \tag{3}$$

**Step 3: Combine.**

From (1), (2), and (3):
$$\sum_{(c)} \varepsilon(c_{(2)})\, \Delta(c_{(1)}) = \Delta(c) \quad \text{in } C \otimes C.$$

Under the natural isomorphism $\mathbb{k} \otimes (C \otimes C) \xrightarrow{\sim} C \otimes C$, $\lambda \otimes y \mapsto \lambda \cdot y$, the element $\sum_{(c)} \varepsilon(c_{(2)}) \otimes \Delta(c_{(1)}) \in \mathbb{k} \otimes (C \otimes C)$ maps exactly to $\sum_{(c)} \varepsilon(c_{(2)})\, \Delta(c_{(1)}) \in C \otimes C$. Therefore, identifying $\mathbb{k} \otimes (C \otimes C) \cong C \otimes C$,
$$\sum_{(c)} \varepsilon(c_{(2)}) \otimes \Delta(c_{(1)}) = \Delta(c). \qquad \blacksquare$$

